        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ============================================================================
         SCRUM-107: PAI NO LONGER MINTS ITS READ SOURCES, AND THAT IS AN ORDERING
         CONTRACT.

         000-bootstrap.xml is deleted. It bootstrap-created three relations PAI does
         not own, in unguarded raw-SQL blocks, so that PAI could run on a fresh
         database before their owners ever had:

           tx_header, tx_entry ...... PRR owns them (R-04 single writer)
           account .................. nobody in this family owns it; the acs service
                                      is being built to own it, in dcre_acs

         A reader minting the writer's schema is the shared-table coupling the family
         split exists to remove, and it is not merely untidy. PRR's baseline creates
         tx_header and tx_entry UNGUARDED, so a PAI mint that won the race would make
         PRR crashloop on "relation already exists". The mint was also a hand-copied
         mirror that nothing compared against the owner, which is a drift class: PAI's
         suite would stay green against a column set PRR had already changed. This is
         the same retirement collections/crg performed, copied rather than reinvented.

         THE CONTRACT: PAI's migration must run AFTER PRR's. On a fresh dcre_pay, PAI
         migrating first now fails loudly and immediately, naming the missing relation
         instead of inventing a wrong one. That is the correct failure.

         THE WRITE SIDE IS NOW CLOSED. PAI issued INSERT INTO account against its
         primary datasource, which is a write-ownership violation whichever database
         that datasource points at, and no published view can fix it: a context that
         cannot control writes to a relation does not own it. Those writes were
         synthetic sentinels with hardcoded toolkit-sample values, not reference data,
         so they moved to a PAI-owned relation named for what they mean,
         unknown_creditor (003-unknown-creditor.xml). Nothing here presupposes where
         the account master ends up.

         THE OPEN EDGE that remains, recorded rather than left to be discovered:
         nothing creates account in dcre_pay, and PAI still READS it to decide whether
         a creditor is known. So PAI cannot complete a run against a real dcre_pay
         until the account master has a home and this READ is repointed at it. That is
         deliberately NOT worked around with a mint, because a mint is what put PAI's
         schema in the wrong database in the first place, and it now fails loudly and
         immediately, naming the missing relation. Integration tests stand the read
         sources up from src/test/resources/db/changelog/test/001-read-sources.xml.
         ============================================================================ -->

    <include file="db/changelog/2026/07/001-pai.xml"/>
    <include file="db/changelog/2026/07/002-batch-metadata.xml"/>
    <include file="db/changelog/2026/08/003-unknown-creditor.xml"/>

</databaseChangeLog>
